.TH UBERTOOTH\-SPECAN 1 "October 2026" "Project Ubertooth" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
.PP
ubertooth\-specan(1) \- a spectrum analyzer for Ubertooth

.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
ubertooth\-specan [\-l <freq>] [\-u <freq>] [\-g|G] [\-d <filename>|\-]

.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
ubertooth\-specan(1) is a tool for using Ubertooth as a spectrum analyzer
in the 2.4GHz band. It is often used as a helper for GUI tools, such as
ubertooth\-specan\-ui or Spectools. It can also produce output suitable for
user with the feedgnuplot tool.

.PP
Other options allow upper and lower bounds to be set on the frequencies
monitored. Refer to the [OPTIONS][] section for full details.

.SH OPTIONS
.PP
Options:
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-l <2402\-2480>\fR :
lower frequency in MHz (default 2402); the value is required when \fB\fC\-l\fR is used
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-u <2402\-2480>\fR :
upper frequency in MHz (default 2480); the value is required when \fB\fC\-u\fR is used
and must not be lower than the selected lower frequency
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-g\fR :
format output for feedgnuplot
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-G\fR :
format output for 3D feedgnuplot
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-d<filename>\fR :
write the binary samples to <filename>. A bare \fB\fC\-\fR (\fB\fC\-d \-\fR) writes
them to standard output instead; every other value is a file name, including
one that starts with \fB\fC\-\fR, so \fB\fC\-d \-x\fR writes the file named
\fB\fC\-x\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-v\fR :
print verbose output to stderr
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-U <index|serial>\fR :
select an Ubertooth by enumeration index or serial number; see
.BR ubertooth (7),
SELECTING A DEVICE

.PP
.SH EXAMPLES

.PP
To monitor the 2.4GHz band and produce a file suitable for feedgnuplot:

.PP
ubertooth\-specan \-g \-d output.dat

.PP
To monitor the upper half of the 2.4GHz band, use:

.PP
ubertooth\-specan \-l 2440

.SH ARGUMENTS
.PP
\fB\fCubertooth\-specan\fR takes options only. A word that is not an option, including
a bare \fB\fC\-\fR, is refused before any device is opened and named:
\fB\fCerror: unexpected argument '<word>'\fR, with exit status 1.
.PP
\fB\fC\-h\fR ends the command only once the whole command line has
parsed, so a stray word or a bad option value is reported even
alongside it.
.PP
An option value must be a whole value in its range, or it is refused the same
way instead of being read as 0, for example
\fB\fCerror: lower frequency must be 2402\-2480 MHz (got bad)\fR\&.
.PP
\fB\fC\-\fR means standard output only as the whole value of \fB\fC\-d\fR\&. A
value that merely starts with \fB\fC\-\fR is a file name, so a forgotten file
name such as \fB\fC\-d \-v\fR writes a file called \fB\fC\-v\fR rather than
streaming samples to the terminal and dropping the \fB\fC\-v\fR\&.
